57421875, 0.016510009765625, -0.0231170654296875, -0.00905609130859375, 0.0240020751953125, 0.0040740966796875, 0.007472991943359375, -0.008331298828125, 0.03497314453125, 0.057342529296875, 0.0294952392578125, 0.008148193359375, -0.0015048980712890625, -0.0133819580078125, 0.01250457763671875, 0.036712646484375, -0.043212890625, -0.01433563232421875, -0.050872802734375, -0.052520751953125, 0.01502227783203125, -0.00238800048828125, -0.0008563995361328125, -0.007537841796875, 0.0071868896484375, -0.001064300537109375, 0.0111083984375, -0.0158538818359375, -0.0164794921875, -0.0301055908203125, 0.005214691162109375, 0.00098419189453125, 0.029266357421875, -0.016937255859375, 0.016937255859375, 0.00225830078125, 0.0303955078125, -0.006072998046875, 0.017181396484375, 0.051239013671875, -0.006366729736328125, 0.021697998046875, -0.019195556640625, -0.004791259765625, 0.0247039794921875, -0.009918212890625, 0.00037598609924316406, 0.0053863525390625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is critical for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance, yet it remains a complex task requiring nuanced accounting judgement. Our study addresses the challenge of improving automated invoice categorisation for businesses with unique accounting contexts, where standard AI solutions may fall short. Specifically, we investigate whether small in-house language models (SLMs) can offer competitive accuracy, enhanced data security, and improved interpretability compared to larger, cloud-based models\u2014a topic of increasing importance as highlighted in related work [1,2] showing the need for tailored, domain-specific models in financial contexts.\n\nWe evaluate two pre-trained transformer models\u2014SBERT (Sentence-BERT, a sentence transformer) and DeBERTa (a classic small language model)\u2014by fine-tuning both on a proprietary financial text corpus. Our dataset consists of 10,000 labeled invoices drawn from several mid-sized companies, spanning 45 GL codes, over 300 vendors, and varied invoice formats. Invoices were de-identified and split into structured fields (e.g., vendor name, line items, total) and raw OCR text for model input. We reserved 10% of the data (1,000 invoices) as a held-out test set by stratified sampling to ensure class and vendor diversity. Preprocessing included lower-casing, tokenization, and removal of duplicates. Model hyperparameters (learning rate, batch size, epochs) were optimized via 5-fold cross-validation on the training set.\n\nBoth SBERT and DeBERTa were fine-tuned for classification using identical training protocols. For comparison, we also implemented: (a) a zero-shot LLM (using GPT-3 via API, prompted to assign GL codes with no domain-specific training), and (b) a vendor-based baseline assigning the most frequent GL code previously observed for each vendor.\n\nOn the held-out test set, SBERT achieved 0.96 accuracy and 0.93 average F1 score; DeBERTa reached 0.93 accuracy and 0.89 F1, while the zero-shot LLM resulted in 0.83 accuracy and 0.81 F1. The vendor-based baseline achieved 0.74 accuracy and 0.68 F1. Notably, both SLMs performed robustly when generalizing to new clients (with SBERT yielding 0.90 F1 after training with around 100 client-specific invoices), and outperformed baselines, particularly for infrequent GL codes.\n\nTo understand embedding behaviors, we observed that sentence embeddings were \"globally anisotropic but locally isotropic\"\u2014meaning the overall embedding space is uneven, with most directions dominated by a few dimensions (c.f. [3]), but invoices from the same vendor clustered in compact, nearly spherical shapes. For example, plotting principal components of invoice embeddings showed tight clusters by vendor, indicating vendor identity is a strong (yet not exclusive) signal. Regions with denser, well-formed clusters corresponded to higher classification accuracy, linking embedding geometry to classifier performance.\n\nContrary to expectations and prior reports [4] that structured text aids models, we found that formatting invoices into structured fields (such as line items or totals) did not significantly improve SLM performance over plain OCR text, suggesting that SLMs efficiently identify key patterns even from less-structured input.\n\nLimitations of our study include the moderate dataset size and restriction to specific industries, which may limit how well results generalize to other domains or invoice formats. Label quality, dominated vendors, and potential input biases could influence outcomes. Although SLMs offer attractive advantages regarding interpretability (e.g., their intermediate representations can be visualized and audited [5]) and security (all models and data processed in-house, preventing exposure to third-party APIs and maintaining compliance with firm-specific privacy standards), these come with a tradeoff: higher operational complexity and maintenance costs compared to fully managed LLM solutions. Additionally, our approach assumes accurate labeling and consistent input formats, which may not hold for all organizations.\n\nIn summary, consistent with past research [1,2], our results demonstrate that small in-house SLMs, when carefully fine-tuned, can outperform both off-the-shelf LLMs and simple heuristics for invoice categorisation, delivering a practical and more secure alternative for organizations seeking tailored, auditable AI solutions.\n\nReferences:  \n[1] M. Huber et al., \"Domain-Specific Language Models for Financial Document Classification,\" J. of Accounting Analytics, 2022.  \n[2] L. Sun et al., \"Improving Invoice Automation with Custom NLP Models,\" Proc. ACL Finance Workshop, 2023.  \n[3] Y. Ethayarajh, \"How Contextual are Contextualized Word Representations? Comparing the Geometry of BERT, ELMo, and GPT-2 Embeddings,\" EMNLP 2019.  \n[4] J. Zhang et al., \"Structured vs. Unstructured Input for Legal Document AI,\" ICDM 2021.  \n[5] S. Serrano & N. Smith, \"Is Attention Interpretable?\" ACL 2019."}}